//     Capacity must be positive. Zero or negative capacity returns an error.
//   - Submit(ctx context.Context, task Task) (<-chan Result, error): Submits a task
//     to the pool. Returns a channel that will receive exactly one Result when the
//     task completes, is canceled, or the pool is shut down. If the pool is full,
//     this blocks until a worker is available or ctx is canceled. If the pool is
//     closed, returns ErrPoolClosed. If ctx is already canceled, returns ErrContextCanceled.
//   - Shutdown(ctx context.Context) error: Initiates graceful shutdown. No new tasks
//     are accepted. Existing tasks continue to run. Returns when all workers have
//     finished or ctx is canceled. If ctx is canceled before all workers finish,
//     returns ErrContextCanceled.
//   - Close(): Immediately closes the pool. All pending tasks are canceled, and
//     their result channels receive a Result with ErrPoolClosed. Workers exit.
//     Safe to call multiple times.
//
// Concurrency invariants:
//   - At most `capacity` tasks are executing concurrently.
//   - Each submitted task produces exactly one Result on its channel.
//   - No goroutine leaks: all worker goroutines exit after Close() or Shutdown().
//   - No data races: all shared state is protected by mutexes or atomic operations.
//   - Panic in a task is recovered and delivered as an error in the Result.
//   - Backpressure: Submit blocks when the pool is at capacity until a worker frees up.
//   - Cancellation: If the context passed to Submit is canceled before the task starts,
//     the task is not executed and the result channel receives ErrContextCanceled.
//   - If the context passed to a task is canceled during execution, the task should
//     respect it and return; the pool does not forcibly kill tasks.
//   - Shutdown waits for all in-flight tasks to complete. Close cancels all pending
//     and in-flight tasks.
//   - Repeated Close() is safe and idempotent.
//   - Submit after Close() returns ErrPoolClosed.
//   - Submit after Shutdown() has started but before all workers exit: if the pool
//     is still accepting (i.e., Shutdown hasn't fully completed), Submit may succeed
//     if capacity is available. Once Shutdown is initiated, no new tasks are accepted.
//     Actually, per design: Shutdown prevents new submissions. So Submit after
//     Shutdown() call returns ErrPoolClosed.
//
// Edge cases handled:
//   - Zero or negative capacity: NewPool returns an error.
//   - Repeated Close(): Safe, no panic.
//   - Task panics: Recovered, error delivered.
//   - Canceled waiters: If Submit's ctx is canceled while waiting for capacity,
//     returns ErrContextCanceled and the task is not enqueued.
//   - Resources released during cleanup: All worker goroutines exit, channels closed.
//   - Loader/task failures: Task errors are delivered via Result.Err.
//   - Canceled waiters during Shutdown: Shutdown returns ErrContextCanceled if ctx
//     is canceled before all workers finish.
//
// Tradeoffs:
//   - Backpressure is implemented via blocking on a semaphore-like mechanism. This
//     is simple and correct but may lead to goroutine blocking under high load.
//   - Deterministic result delivery is achieved by ensuring each task has a dedicated
//     result channel. This uses more memory per task but guarantees ordering and
//     no lost results.
//   - Panic recovery is per-task; a panicking task does not crash the pool.
//   - Shutdown is graceful: waits for in-flight tasks. Close is abrupt: cancels all.
//   - The pool does not support dynamic resizing of capacity.

